From: mklab <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH 46/46] TODO 17.4: number() now agrees with C99, in the two places it did not

Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc

Two divergences had been asserted AS THEMSELVES since 2026-08-26, so that closing
either one would go red on purpose. Both are closed.

  case          C99         before      after
  %.0d of 0     (nothing)   "0"         (nothing)
  %08.3d of 42  "     042"  "00000042"  "     042"

C99 7.19.6.1p8 says "if the value and the precision are both 0, no characters
are produced"; number() wrote '0' unconditionally. C99 7.19.6.1p6 says "for d,
i, o, u, x, and X conversions, if a precision is specified, the 0 flag is
ignored"; number() did not ignore it. GCC corroborates the second independently
-- -Werror=format= refuses the literal with "'0' flag ignored with precision and
'%d'".

THE PARKED REASONING WAS RE-DERIVED, NOT TRUSTED, and it still holds: a repo-wide
grep for an integer conversion carrying a precision finds ppc's four %8.8lx in
the boot path -- which carry no 0 flag and are asserted correct -- and, for
%08.3d, nothing but the fixture asserting the divergence itself. So there is
still no in-tree caller.

WHAT THE PARKED NOTE DID NOT WEIGH is §13.3(C), which happened in this same tree:
a printf that disagrees with C99 is a latent "two printfs answering one call".
GCC may constant-fold snprintf's RETURN per C99 while our code writes different
bytes -- exactly the ppc32 bug that cost a day and presented as a firmware
defect. -fno-builtin now prevents it on every arch (patch 29), but that is a
mitigation one build-flag edit away from being lost, and the divergence is the
thing being mitigated. Closing it removes the hazard rather than the symptom.

THIRD FIX, and it is what makes the second fixture legitimate: number() did
`num = -num` on a long long with no guard, so LLONG_MIN was UNDEFINED BEHAVIOUR.
§13.3(B) named it from the source and declined to test it "because testing it
would mean shipping the UB in a fixture", which was right. The digits now come
from an unsigned accumulator -- converting to unsigned is defined (C99 6.3.1.3p2)
and negating an unsigned is modular -- so the magnitude is correct for every
input, and an llmin case can be shipped.

AND THAT CASE IS AN UNKNOWN, NOT A PROOF, which was measured rather than assumed.
Re-injecting the old signed `-num` leaves the llmin line GREEN: on x86-64 GCC the
undefined negation happens to produce the two's-complement bit pattern, which is
the right magnitude. A test cannot observe the ABSENCE of undefined behaviour --
that is a property of the source. So the fixture asserts the output and the note
says what it cannot say. Recorded in TODO 17.4 rather than folded into the pass.

The two divergence fixtures INVERT rather than disappear -- they were built for
exactly this day -- and move from the divergence counter into the ordinary one:
14 + 2 + 1 = 17/17, 0/0 recorded divergence. The "0/0" half stays in the summary
on purpose: it is a positive statement that somebody looked, and dropping the
phrase would make a tree carrying a NEW divergence print what a conformant one
prints.

Controls, all re-injected and watched:
  * %.0d of 0 writes '0' again      -> d-zero  want[](0) got[0](1) BAD
  * the 0 flag survives a precision -> zeropad-prec got[00000042] BAD
  * the signed negation restored    -> BLIND, reported above rather than hidden

